# google-services.json: GitHub's secret scanner flags and has confirmed-publicly-leaked its
# api_key (2 alerts, both "Google API Key", both publicly_leaked — see the repo's Security >
# Secret scanning tab). Google's own stance is that this key doesn't need to be secret IF it has
# Android application restrictions (package name + signing SHA-1) set in Google Cloud Console —
# but untracking it here stops re-leaking whatever value is in it, whether or not those
# restrictions are set, and matches the README's own setup instructions ("add your
# google-services.json to app/" — i.e. each developer/box supplies its own, it is not shipped in
# the repo). The already-leaked key values still need rotating in Firebase console regardless;
# removing this from git does not undo that they were public.
app/google-services.json
*.keystore
*.jks
# Debug signing key is checked in deliberately: it's the standard well-known-password Android
# debug keystore, never used for the production release identity, and must be stable (not
# freshly regenerated per machine/CI run) so Digital Asset Links / passkey origin checks against
# the beta app's fixed fingerprint keep working.
!app/debug.keystore
*.hprof
